Our calendar app shows back-to-back busy blocks as two separate blocks when they should merge into one; pin it down with fast-check property tests. Save /workspace/intervals.mjs:

export function mergeIntervals(intervals) {
  // intervals: array of [start, end] with start <= end; return merged, sorted, non-overlapping
  if (intervals.length === 0) return [];
  const sorted = intervals.slice().sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
  const out = [sorted[0].slice()];
  for (let i = 1; i < sorted.length; i++) {
    const [s, e] = sorted[i];
    const last = out[out.length - 1];
    if (s < last[1]) {
      last[1] = Math.max(last[1], e);
    } else {
      out.push([s, e]);
    }
  }
  return out;
}

The touching-intervals case is wrong: [1,2] and [2,3] should merge to [1,3] but the s < last[1] comparison keeps them separate. npm install fast-check, write intervals.test.mjs (node:test) with properties: output is sorted and strictly non-overlapping AND non-touching (gap > 0 between consecutive intervals); every input point set membership is preserved (check with a brute-force integer membership oracle over intervals drawn from small integer ranges, fc.integer bounds -50 to 50); output covers exactly the union. Run, let fast-check find the [ [1,2], [2,3] ] class of counterexample, record it as a fixed regression case, fix the comparison, and rerun with 500 runs green.

